edit(p,'Real hardware and compilers do not provide it by default, because it would make them much slower.','Uncoordinated accesses do not generally provide that model; language rules and hardware ordering permit optimizations. The performance effect of stronger ordering depends on the target and workload.','Unsupporteduniversalperformancedifference.',s)
edit(p,'Atomicity solves "half-done" operations.','For some atomic types, an implementation may use internal locks; the atomic API does not universally promise lock-free machine instructions. Atomicity solves "half-done" operations.','C atomicsnotalllockfree.',s)
edit(p,'Different architectures allow different reorderings:','This simplified table concerns ordinary accesses to coherent normal cacheable memory, not every memory type, special instruction or dependency rule:', 'x86NT/WC/MMIOandARMdependencies exceptions.',hw)
edit(p,'The fix is a **full fence** between the store and the load (`mfence` on x86), which forces the store buffer to drain first.','In the machine-code model, a full fence between the store and load in each thread forbids that result (for example mfence on x86). In C, first use valid atomic accesses; a hardware fence does not legalize races on plain objects.','Needbothfencesandvalidlanguageatomics.',s+hw)
edit(p,'With relaxed ordering, the store to `data` may become visible after the store to `ready`, or the load of `data` may be satisfied before the load of `ready`. On ARM this can genuinely print 0. The compiler is also allowed to reorder the two stores on any architecture. (Strictly, reading `data` here is also a data race, because nothing orders it after the write.)','The plain data accesses lack a happens-before edge and therefore race: the C program has undefined behavior, not merely a choice between 0 and 42. Weakly ordered machine traces explain one possible stale-read symptom but do not bound C behavior.', 'UBprimarynotafterthoughtpossiblevalues.',s)
edit(p,'Every mutex implementation has exactly these semantics, which is why code that protects all shared data with locks never has to think about memory ordering.','Correct standard mutex use supplies the synchronization needed for protected accesses. Applications must still coordinate object lifetime, initialization and all access paths; having a lock somewhere is not enough.','Everymutexexactly/neverthinktoobroad.',s)
edit(p,'It is the only one of these that forbids the store-buffering outcome.','Using seq_cst for all four operations in this litmus test forbids both loads seeing zero. Other correctly constructed synchronization protocols or fence placements can also forbid it.', 'Seqcstnotonlypossibleprotocol.',s)
edit(p,'Locking every call is slow, so programmers wrote:','To avoid locking an already-initialized fast path, programmers wrote:', 'Unsupportedslowclaim.',s)
edit(p,'It is broken. Line 3','It is broken: the unlocked plain pointer read races with publication in C. One illustrative failure is incomplete publication. Line 3','ExplicitpointerUB.',s)
edit(p,'The free-threaded build keeps built-in types internally consistent, but compound operations still need locks. In both cases: **use `threading` primitives and `queue.Queue`, and you inherit their happens-before guarantees.**','Free-threaded CPython currently uses internal locking for many built-ins, but that is not a blanket language-level atomicity contract. In both cases use documented threading and queue protocols for shared invariants. Python does not specify the complete C11 memory model described above.', 'DontclaimformalPythonHB/C11equivalence.',['https://docs.python.org/3/howto/free-threading-python.html'])
edit(p,'and is the only ordering that rules out the store-buffering outcome','and rules out both-zero in this test when all four operations use it','Matchqualifiedlesson.',s)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q1','prompt','Assume int can represent 800000 and hits is initialized to zero before starting eight threads. Each increments it 100000 times and all are joined before reading the result. Which declaration/update gives 800000 without an application mutex?','Missinginitialization/resultobservation/rangeandnonlockfree caveat.',s)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q2','text','Volatile accesses follow implementation-defined observable-access rules; volatile alone gives no inter-thread atomicity or synchronization','Standarddoesnotguaranteeeachsourceaccessexactphysicalmemory.',v,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q2','explanation','Correct. Volatile is useful for supported hardware/signal conventions, but is not a replacement for C atomic types or locks. The implementation defines what constitutes a volatile access.','Exactstandardqualification.',v,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q3','explanation','Coherence alone cannot repair a compiler-hoisted load or a C data race. No fixed propagation time is guaranteed here.','Removeunsupportednanoseconds.',hw,option=0)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q5','prompt','Assume one producer, one consumer, initialized globals, no other accesses, and that the consumer reaches printf. What value does it print?\n\n```c\nint data = 0;\natomic_int ready = 0;\n\n// producer\ndata = 42;\natomic_store_explicit(&ready, 1,\n    memory_order_release);\n// consumer\nwhile (!atomic_load_explicit(&ready,\n        memory_order_acquire)) ;\nprintf("%d", data);\n```','Visibilityresultnotprogressguarantee.',s)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q5','explanation','The release/acquire pair orders the plain data accesses. Replacing it with relaxed access would introduce a C data race, not a defined two-value outcome.','RelaxedplainprogramUB.',s,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q5','explanation','Correct C release/acquire synchronization provides the result on conforming targets. The relaxed plain-data version is undefined behavior on either architecture.','Removeincorrectrelaxedx8642guarantee.',s,option=3)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q6','explanation','Acquire is a valid RMW order, but it is stronger than necessary here. The joins supply the required final-observation ordering; no producer publication is needed from the increments.','AcquireRMWnotmeaninglessin general.',s,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q11','text','No: both-zero remains allowed; use all seq_cst operations or a correctly placed seq_cst fence between store and load in each thread','Singularfencecouldmisleadone-sidedfix.',s,option=3)
qe(q,'sync-memory-ordering-q11','explanation','Correct. If both acquire loads read the initial zero, neither reads the other release, so no such synchronizes-with edge connects them. All-seq_cst operations forbid that cycle; properly placed fences can also do so.','LogicalHBreasonvsimplementationreordering.',s,option=3)
